WEBVTT

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:07.832
Welcome to CSC4801, AI-assisted Software Engineering.

00:00:07.832 --> 00:00:12.020
This is Lecture Seven: Requirements Exercise.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:22.301
Our course follows the software life cycle: requirement engineering, coding, testing and verification, repair, and finally AI ops.

00:00:22.301 --> 00:00:24.640
We are still at the first stage.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:28.174
Requirement engineering itself is a cycle.

00:00:28.174 --> 00:00:38.030
We elicit requirements from people, model them, write them down as a specification, validate them, and manage them as they change.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.643
Today we ask a practical question.

00:00:40.643 --> 00:00:47.300
What happens when the reader of a requirement document is not a human developer, but a coding agent?

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:50.499
Requirement documents have real strengths here.

00:00:50.499 --> 00:00:55.530
First, they force us to think before we act, instead of jumping straight into code.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:01:00.502
Second, they fit the software engineering life cycle we already know.

00:01:00.502 --> 00:01:04.120
Design, tests, and reviews can all point back to them.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.788
And third, they help us navigate AI agents.

00:01:07.788 --> 00:01:15.350
A written requirement is a map: it tells the agent where to go, and tells us whether it got there.

00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:17.809
But there is a weakness.

00:01:17.809 --> 00:01:24.093
Requirements are usually written in informal language, and informal language is ambiguous.

00:01:24.443 --> 00:01:25.884
Take this requirement.

00:01:25.884 --> 00:01:31.123
If the user's balance is insufficient, the system should reject the transaction.

00:01:31.473 --> 00:01:32.503
Pause and think.

00:01:32.503 --> 00:01:37.713
If the balance is exactly equal to the amount, should the transaction go through?

00:01:38.263 --> 00:01:42.707
One reading says reject when the balance is less than the amount.

00:01:42.707 --> 00:01:46.536
Another says reject when it is less than or equal to it.

00:01:46.536 --> 00:01:47.903
Both are reasonable.

00:01:48.253 --> 00:01:51.624
A human developer might walk over and ask.

00:01:51.624 --> 00:01:56.840
An agent usually just picks one, quietly, and writes code for it.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:00.933
The ambiguity becomes a bug that nobody decided on.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:07.300
So how do we use requirement documents with coding agents, without falling into this trap?

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.829
One answer is spec-driven development.

00:02:10.829 --> 00:02:19.530
It is a methodology for the LLM era: write a clear and detailed specification before any implementation.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.836
Its supporters make a bold argument.

00:02:22.836 --> 00:02:26.941
The specification is more important than the code.

00:02:26.941 --> 00:02:33.920
When an agent can regenerate code quickly, the spec becomes the real source of truth.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.489
One popular toolkit for this is called Spec Kit, from GitHub.

00:02:38.489 --> 00:02:44.800
It works with many AI coding agents, and turns spec-driven development into a series of commands.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.426
Its life cycle has 5 stages.

00:02:47.426 --> 00:02:50.109
The constitution sets guardrails.

00:02:50.109 --> 00:02:52.547
Specify defines what to build.

00:02:52.547 --> 00:02:54.904
Plan designs how to build it.

00:02:54.904 --> 00:02:58.156
Tasks breaks the work into atomic units.

00:02:58.156 --> 00:03:00.350
And implement just does it.

00:03:01.134 --> 00:03:03.144
Stage 1 is the constitution.

00:03:03.144 --> 00:03:06.374
It sets the principles for the whole project.

00:03:06.724 --> 00:03:13.324
That includes the technical stack and architecture, security and compliance standards, and performance benchmarks.

00:03:13.674 --> 00:03:22.714
It is saved in the project folder, in a file called constitution.md, so every later step can read it.

00:03:23.064 --> 00:03:24.372
Here is an example.

00:03:24.372 --> 00:03:31.260
This project follows a library-first approach: every feature is built as a standalone library first.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.015
We use test-driven development strictly.

00:03:34.015 --> 00:03:37.184
And we prefer functional programming patterns.

00:03:37.534 --> 00:03:40.212
These principles then become gates.

00:03:40.212 --> 00:03:44.039
The plan template turns each one into a check box.

00:03:44.039 --> 00:03:48.707
Is the feature a standalone library? Are tests written first?

00:03:48.707 --> 00:03:52.534
A plan must pass these checks before work goes on.

00:03:53.334 --> 00:03:56.034
Stage 2 is the specification.

00:03:56.034 --> 00:03:59.014
A good spec answers 3 questions.

00:03:59.364 --> 00:04:03.724
Outcomes: what should the system achieve when the work is finished?

00:04:04.074 --> 00:04:08.614
Boundaries: what is in scope, and what is explicitly out of scope.

00:04:08.614 --> 00:04:13.154
This stops the agent from creeping into features nobody asked for.

00:04:13.504 --> 00:04:18.584
Constraints: earlier decisions and technical limits the agent must respect.

00:04:18.934 --> 00:04:21.872
Our running example is a photo organizer.

00:04:21.872 --> 00:04:27.105
Build an application that helps me organize my photos in separate albums.

00:04:27.105 --> 00:04:33.054
Albums are grouped by date, and can be reordered by drag and drop on the main page.

00:04:33.404 --> 00:04:36.387
Albums are never nested inside other albums.

00:04:36.387 --> 00:04:40.524
And inside each album, photos are previewed in a tile layout.

00:04:40.874 --> 00:04:44.428
Notice what this prompt does not say.

00:04:44.428 --> 00:04:48.175
No language, no framework, no database.

00:04:48.175 --> 00:04:52.114
The spec describes what and why, not how.

00:04:52.714 --> 00:04:57.503
The toolkit also writes a quality checklist for the spec itself.

00:04:57.503 --> 00:05:06.034
Does it avoid implementation details? Are the requirements testable and unambiguous? Is the scope clearly bounded?

00:05:06.834 --> 00:05:09.183
Stage 3 is the technical plan.

00:05:09.183 --> 00:05:14.274
The plan file is the bridge between business intent and raw code.

00:05:14.624 --> 00:05:22.464
It defines data models and schemas, API contracts and endpoints, the component architecture, and the tech stack.

00:05:22.814 --> 00:05:27.987
For our photo organizer, we say: use Vite, with as few libraries as possible.

00:05:27.987 --> 00:05:31.414
Use vanilla HTML, CSS, and JavaScript where we can.

00:05:31.764 --> 00:05:38.804
Images are never uploaded anywhere, and metadata is stored in a local SQLite database.

00:05:39.154 --> 00:05:43.147
Now the how is written down too, but in a separate file.

00:05:43.147 --> 00:05:47.354
If we later change the tech stack, the spec stays the same.

00:05:48.134 --> 00:05:52.390
Before breaking down the work, Spec Kit can generate a checklist.

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:55.534
Think of it as unit tests for your requirements.

00:05:55.884 --> 00:06:00.871
It asks questions such as: are all the main user journeys documented?

00:06:00.871 --> 00:06:06.004
Are edge cases defined, like photos with no date, or very large albums?

00:06:06.354 --> 00:06:13.554
The goal is to confirm that the spec is complete, clear, and consistent, before any code exists.

00:06:13.904 --> 00:06:15.385
Next come the tasks.

00:06:15.385 --> 00:06:21.384
The plan is broken into atomic units, each one a small change that can be tested.

00:06:21.734 --> 00:06:29.214
This keeps the agent focused on one change at a time, and every change is checked against the original spec.

00:06:29.814 --> 00:06:40.774
At this point, the feature folder contains the whole set: spec, plan, and tasks, plus a data model, API contracts, research notes, and the checklist.

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:44.876
With that many documents, they can drift apart.

00:06:44.876 --> 00:06:46.847
So there is an analyze step.

00:06:47.197 --> 00:06:55.307
It reads the spec, the plan, and the tasks together, and reports conflicts, gaps, and ambiguities across them.

00:06:55.307 --> 00:06:58.477
It only reads; it does not change any file.

00:06:58.827 --> 00:07:00.926
Only then comes implementation.

00:07:00.926 --> 00:07:05.667
The agent takes the task list and writes the code, one task at a time.

00:07:06.017 --> 00:07:08.003
How is all this built?

00:07:08.003 --> 00:07:19.377
Each command is packaged as an agent skill: a folder with instructions for one step, such as specify, plan, tasks, or analyze.

00:07:19.727 --> 00:07:21.213
So there is no magic.

00:07:21.213 --> 00:07:28.927
Spec-driven development is a set of well-written prompts, plus a discipline about which document comes first.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:30.826
Now, the exercise.

00:07:30.826 --> 00:07:35.580
You can practice spec-driven development yourself, even without the toolkit.

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:44.690
There are 2 goals: use the Spec Kit workflow to analyze requirements, and find inconsistencies in a set of requirements.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:49.345
Let's walk through the 3 files for a course registration system.

00:07:49.345 --> 00:07:50.960
First, the constitution.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:59.503
Tech stack: React and TypeScript on the front end, Node and Express on the back end, and PostgreSQL.

00:07:59.503 --> 00:08:03.927
Principles: library-first, test-driven, and API-first.

00:08:03.927 --> 00:08:09.990
Security: no hard-coded credentials, validate every input, and HTTPS only.

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:11.572
Second, the spec.

00:08:11.572 --> 00:08:20.340
Outcomes: students search and register for courses, professors manage rosters, and admins configure registration periods.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:24.336
Out of scope: payments, grades, and attendance.

00:08:24.336 --> 00:08:30.930
Constraints: support 1,000 concurrent users, and use the university's single sign-on.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:32.543
Third, the plan.

00:08:32.543 --> 00:08:36.334
Data models for course, student, and enrollment.

00:08:36.334 --> 00:08:40.913
Endpoints to search courses, register, and fetch a roster.

00:08:40.913 --> 00:08:43.440
And the components of each page.

00:08:44.233 --> 00:08:48.990
The second part is the heart of the exercise: requirement verification.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:52.473
Here are 6 requirements for a smart home controller.

00:08:52.823 --> 00:08:57.045
1: the system shall control all lights in the house.

00:08:57.045 --> 00:09:00.130
2: it shall respond to voice commands.

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:03.783
3: it shall learn user preferences over time.

00:09:04.133 --> 00:09:08.357
4: it shall work without an internet connection.

00:09:08.357 --> 00:09:12.669
5: it shall integrate with Alexa and Google Home.

00:09:12.669 --> 00:09:16.013
6: it shall be affordable, under $100.

00:09:16.363 --> 00:09:17.056
Your turn.

00:09:17.056 --> 00:09:20.936
Pause the video now, and find as many issues as you can.

00:09:20.936 --> 00:09:28.003
Look for words you can't test, requirements that fight each other, and things that are simply missing.

00:09:28.553 --> 00:09:28.858
Ready?

00:09:28.858 --> 00:09:30.433
Let's go through them together.

00:09:31.233 --> 00:09:34.393
It helps to sort the issues into categories.

00:09:34.743 --> 00:09:35.483
Ambiguity.

00:09:35.483 --> 00:09:37.703
Requirement 6 says affordable.

00:09:37.703 --> 00:09:39.108
Affordable to whom?

00:09:39.108 --> 00:09:43.103
A student and a landlord would give different answers.

00:09:43.453 --> 00:09:44.268
Conflict.

00:09:44.268 --> 00:09:48.706
Requirement 4 says it works without the internet.

00:09:48.706 --> 00:09:54.139
But Requirement 5 needs Alexa, and Alexa needs the internet.

00:09:54.139 --> 00:09:56.493
Both cannot be fully true.

00:09:57.093 --> 00:09:57.649
Missing.

00:09:57.649 --> 00:10:00.079
What happens during a power outage?

00:10:00.079 --> 00:10:02.093
Nothing in the list tells us.

00:10:02.443 --> 00:10:03.489
Unmeasurable.

00:10:03.489 --> 00:10:07.269
Learn user preferences: how much, and how fast?

00:10:07.269 --> 00:10:09.923
There is no way to say it passed.

00:10:10.273 --> 00:10:11.118
Incomplete.

00:10:11.118 --> 00:10:14.191
There is no security requirement at all.

00:10:14.191 --> 00:10:17.033
What if someone hacks into the house?

00:10:17.383 --> 00:10:18.978
Did you find others?

00:10:18.978 --> 00:10:23.521
All lights is vague too: which lights, from which brands?

00:10:23.521 --> 00:10:27.109
And which voice commands, in which languages?

00:10:27.109 --> 00:10:28.623
Good catches count.

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:31.619
Finding issues is half the job.

00:10:31.619 --> 00:10:37.560
Now we rewrite each requirement to be specific, consistent, complete, and testable.

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:43.046
Specific means replacing vague words with measurable criteria.

00:10:43.046 --> 00:10:47.021
Consistent means resolving conflicts explicitly.

00:10:47.021 --> 00:10:50.169
Complete means adding what is missing.

00:10:50.169 --> 00:10:54.310
Testable means every requirement has a clear test.

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:56.376
Take Requirement 6.

00:10:56.376 --> 00:11:00.980
Before: the system shall be affordable, under $100.

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:07.751
After: the total cost of hardware and software shall not exceed 100 US dollars,

00:11:07.751 --> 00:11:11.084
at retail pricing, as of the launch date.

00:11:11.084 --> 00:11:16.610
And we name the test: a bill of materials review, and a price check.

00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:21.068
Then run the LLM verification again on the new set.

00:11:21.068 --> 00:11:23.261
Did you introduce new issues?

00:11:23.261 --> 00:11:25.530
Are all the old ones resolved?

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:28.880
This is the verification loop.

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:34.480
You write, you check, you fix, and then you check again.

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:37.528
Some tools build this whole workflow in.

00:11:37.528 --> 00:11:42.240
Kiro, for example, is a coding agent designed around spec-driven development.

00:11:43.034 --> 00:11:44.394
Let's sum up.

00:11:44.744 --> 00:11:49.179
Requirement documents help agents: they make us think first,

00:11:49.179 --> 00:11:51.397
and they give the agent a map.

00:11:51.397 --> 00:11:56.424
But informal language is ambiguous, and an agent will quietly guess.

00:11:56.774 --> 00:12:00.434
Spec-driven development puts the spec first:

00:12:00.434 --> 00:12:04.677
constitution, specify, plan, tasks, then implement,

00:12:04.677 --> 00:12:08.254
with checklists and analysis to catch gaps.

00:12:08.604 --> 00:12:14.342
And every requirement should be specific, consistent, complete, and testable,

00:12:14.342 --> 00:12:15.684
checked in a loop.

00:12:16.034 --> 00:12:17.970
Here is something to try.

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:21.455
Take a feature request from your own project,

00:12:21.455 --> 00:12:26.490
and ask an agent to list its ambiguities before writing any code.

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:28.194
How many did you miss?

00:12:28.967 --> 00:12:30.781
That's all for this lecture.

00:12:30.781 --> 00:12:34.927
Thank you for watching, and see you in the next class, Coding 1.

